=begin pod

=head1 NAME

Rakudo-Type-Introspection - introspect Raku types

=head1 SYNOPSIS

=begin code :lang<raku>
use Rakudo-Type-Introspection;

my $type = Rakudo::Type.new(Rat);
say $type.parents; # Cool
say $type.does;    # Rational

my $introspection = Rakudo::Type::Introspection.new(CORE::);
say $introspection<Int>.name;  # Int

say parents(IO::Path); # Cool
say does(IO::Path);    # IO
say core(IO::Path);    # v6c
=end code

=head1 DESCRIPTION

The C<Rakudo-Type-Introspection> distribution provides the logic to
introspect Raku types and C<PseudoStash>es.

=head1 CLASSES

=head2 Rakudo::Type

=begin code :lang<raku>
my $type = Rakudo::Type.new(IntStr);
say $type.core;       # v6c
say $type.namespace;  # class
=end code

The C<Rakudo::Type> object collects information about a type. It
can instantiated by specifying a single positional argument to the
C<new> method.

=head3 ATTRIBUTES

The C<Rakudo::Type> class contains the following public attributes,
which can also be specified as named arguments to the C<new> methdod.

=head4 type

The actual type object that was used to create the C<Rakudo::Type>
object.

=head4 namespace

The type of namespace (scope) of the type.  One of the following:

=item class
=item enum
=item grammar
=item module
=item native
=item nativeref
=item package
=item role
=item subset

=head4 name

The name of the type, as introspected with C<.^name>.

=head4 core

The core level in which the type was defined, as a string.  One of the
following:

=item "v6c"
=item "v6d"
=item "v6e"
=item "" - not a core type

=head4 nqp

Boolean, indicating whether the type was created in NQP.

=head4 parents

A C<List> of C<Rakudo::Type> objects of unique parent classes.

=head4 does

A C<List> of C<Rakudo::Type> objects of unique roles (also excluding
any roles that have already been consumed by any parent class).

=head3 METHODS

=head4 gist

=begin code :lang<raku>
say Rakudo::Type.new(IntStr); # class IntStr is Allomorph is Int
=end code

The C<gist> method provides a simple representation of the type, almost
as if written in Raku.

=head2 Rakudo-Type-Introspection

=begin code :lang<raku>
my $introspection = Rakudo::Type::Introspection.new(CORE::);
say $introspection<IO::Path>.does;  # IO
=end code

The C<Rakudo-Type-Introspection> class incorporates the logic to
recursively collect the type information of a given C<PseudoStash>
(with the C<CORE::> C<PseudoStash> taken as the default).

It exposes the information as a C<Map> keyed on the full name of
all the types found in the given C<PseudoStash>, with the value
being the associated C<Rakudo::Type> object.

=head3 method new

The C<new> method additionally takes the following named arguments:

=head4 :implementation-detail

Will include types that have been marked as C<is implementation-detail>
if specified with a trueish value.  Default is to B<not> include
types that are implementation details.

=head4 :nqp

Will include types that originated from NQP code if specified with a
trueish value.  Default is to B<not> include types from NQP.

=head4 :package

Will include C<package> types if specified with a trueish value.
Default is to B<not> include C<package>s.

=head4 :rakuast

Will include C<RakuAST::...> classes if specified with a trueish
value.  Default is to B<not> include C<RakuAST::...> classes.

=head1 EXPORTED SUBROUTINES

Some basic functionality is exported as subroutines.

=head2 parents

=begin code :lang<raku>
say parents(Int); # Cool
dd Int.^mro;      # (Int, Cool, Any, Mu)
=end code

The C<parents> subroutine takes a type object and returns a list of
types of unique parent classes.  This is different from what the
C<.^mro> method returns.  Because C<Cool> is an C<Any>, and C<Any>
is a C<Mu>, the specification of C<is Cool> is enough in a
C<class Foo is Cool> statement.

=head2 does

=begin code :lang<raku>
say does(Int);  # Real
dd Int.^roles;  # (Real, Numeric)
=end code

The C<does> subroutine takes a type object and returns a list of
unique roles that are consumed by the class.  This is different from
what the C<.^roles> method returns.  Because the C<Real> role consumed
the C<Numeric> role, the specification of C<does Real> is enough in a
C<class Bar does Real> statement.

=head2 core

=begin code :lang<raku>
use v6.e.PREVIEW;
dd core(Int);            # "v6c"
dd core(Formatter);      # "v6e"
dd core(class Foo { });  # ""
=end code

The C<core> subroutine takes a type object and returns a string
indicating in which core level the type was defined, or it returns
the empty string if the type could not be found in core.

=head2 namespace

=begin code :lang<raku>
dd namespace(IO);   # "role"
dd namespace(Int);  # "class"
=end code

The C<namespace> subroutine takes a type object and returns a string
indicating the type of namespace (scope) of the type.

=head2 nqp

=begin code :lang<raku>
dd nqp(IO);                   # False
dd nqp(Metamodel::ClassHOW);  # True
=end code

The C<nqp> subroutine takes a type object and returns a C<Bool>
indicating whether or not the type originated from NQP.

